- Blog editing workflow #workflow
    - Status: [🔴 POC] | Mode: [🔀 Hybrid] | Updated: 2026-08-07
    - Purpose
        - A repeatable, multi-pass workflow for editing long-form blog posts
        - Being developed live on notes/blog/20260806_ ("What is mathematics")
        - Will eventually become a set of skills and agents, see agents.log
    - The ordering principle #principle
        - Passes run big to small, cheap to change before expensive
        - Cut or move paragraphs before polishing sentences
        - Fact check before line editing
        - Validate links after the reorder, so dead text is not validated
        - Breaking this order wastes hours

- Phase 0: Setup #phase0
    - The brief has three parts: reader, promise, success criteria
    - Reader: standing profile, set once for the blog, not per piece
        - Interested in the topic, general audience
        - Will sit through technical detail but is not there to learn it
        - Here for the argument and the conversation
        - Lives in brief.log
    - Promise: inferred from the text, not declared up front
        - An agent reads the whole draft and infers the promise in one sentence
        - The human confirms or corrects the inference
        - Rationale: the writer often does not know what the piece promises until it exists
    - Success criteria: derived from the promise plus the standing aim
        - Standing aim for this blog: raise a question rather than answer it
        - The piece should make the reader wonder
        - Multiple perspectives, leaning toward the conflicting ones
        - Standards still apply: correct, fact checked, flowing, supported
        - But it is a blog post, not an analytical report: nothing here enables an executive decision
    - Output: the brief, stored in brief.log, every later pass uses it as the yardstick

- Phase 1: Inventory #phase1
    - Main points: what is the claim
    - Supporting points: what carries the claim
    - Side points: flavor, edge, human detail, what makes this piece this writer's
    - Missing points: main, supporting, or side points that should exist but do not
    - Output: the point map

- Phase 2: Paragraph clarity #phase2
    - What point does each paragraph deliver
    - Flag back-and-forth between points (two threads braided without signaling)
    - Flag rambling: sentences or paragraphs that promise a payoff and never deliver
    - Output: the clarity report

- Phase 3: Spine decision #phase3
    - Human decision, not a pass
    - Which point is the spine, which points are warmup, engine, support
    - Everything after this is downstream of the decision
    - For 20260806_: spine is C (importance is social), B (process is the product) is warmup and emotional engine

- Phase 4: Structure #phase4
    - Hook: opens a conversation, not a paper abstract
    - Conversational roadmap: tells the reader where we are going in a talking voice
    - Reorder: narrative order, human flow, no sales pitch, no dangling promises
    - Payoff ledger: every hook has a payoff, every payoff has a setup
    - Transitions: sections hand off, they do not just stack
    - Title and headers audit: does the title's promise get paid, do headers guide
    - Conclusion: lands the spine, calls back to the opening
    - Output: the structure proposal and the ledger

- Phase 5: Argument integrity #phase5
    - Fact check: every dated, numbered, or named claim (including what specific people said, did, felt, or believed), with sources; a verified claim with no link in the draft is a finding
    - Quote verification: exact wording against the source, for quotation marks and for reported speech without marks (He asked, He wrote, He quotes)
    - Fairness pass: people critiqued are steelmanned first
    - Adversarial pass: strongest counterargument to the spine, answered or conceded
    - Link validation: external URLs, local files, images
    - Provenance labels: firsthand anecdote vs documented fact vs opinion
    - Output: the integrity report

- Phase 6: Line level #phase6
    - Tone consistency pass (editor's eye)
    - Writer voice pass (protect the edge, do not flatten it)
    - Line edit: passive voice, filler, weak verbs
    - Repetition audit: words, phrases, sentence shapes, aphorism budget
    - Jargon audit against the defined reader: explain, earn, or cut
    - Rhythm pass and read-aloud
    - Mechanics: typos, contractions, number style, markdown
    - Output: the line edit

- Phase 7: Reader facing #phase7
    - Unanswered questions section at the end of the draft, removed before publication
    - Cold skeleton read: first line of each paragraph only, does the piece still make sense
    - Accessibility: alt text, meaningful link text, heading hierarchy
    - Takeaway read: a no-context target-audience reader reads the near-final piece and states in 2 to 3 sentences what they got
        - Appended to the draft after a --- separator at the very end, labeled: what I think a general reader would think about this piece
        - This is the gate before publication
        - Removed before publication, same lifecycle as the unanswered-questions section
    - Output: the reader report

- Phase 8: Images #phase8
    - See images.log for the full pass
    - Summary: candidate spots, job per image, source or create, verify, global audit
    - Output: the image plan

- Phase 9: Publication #phase9
    - Metadata: frontmatter, tags, description, slug, filename
    - Permissions: third-party screenshots and figures, attribution or anonymization
    - Build and preview: renders, images load, LaTeX renders
    - Final rendered read, top to bottom in the rendered page
    - Distillation: mine the workspace for durable lessons
        - Zero to three lines that would be true for a post not yet written
        - Append to the global brief.log or workflow.log
        - Raw debates and findings stay in the workspace, never re-read mid-edit
    - Output: the published piece

- Distilled from 20260806_ "What is mathematics" #distillation
    - When the editing agent cannot open images, verify a proposed diagram by text-extracting its source PDF with pdftotext and confirming the page content before embedding. A slide's text can confirm the diagram without a visual check.
    - Reader pass: a proper noun the standing reader may not know is a reader hole even when it carries a link. A one-clause gloss is a low-severity direct fix, not an escalation.
